<title>USA: Heinz to cut workers, take charge - WSJ.</title>
<headline>Heinz to cut workers, take charge - WSJ.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-03-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>H.J. Heinz Co is expected to take a pre-tax charge of more than $500 million in its fiscal fourth quarter ending April 30 and cut its staff, the Wall Street Journal said in its electronic edition Monday.</p>
<p>The company is likely to cut its 43,000-person work force by 6 percent and is planning to sell business that contribute $400 million to $600 million of its $9.1 billion annual sales, the report said, citing people close to the company.</p>
<p>These businesses include poultry and ice cream operations in New Zealand, a fats and oils business in South Korea and a chain of fitness centres, the paper said.</p>
<p>The report said the company will also close manufacturing facilities in North America and Europe and contract other companies to package certain products.</p>
<p>((-- New York newsdesk 212-859-1610))</p>
